Subject: [overlay 0031] R_SetRepeatFrame — the second eye reuses the dlight state, not consumes it

Companion to 0030. With the render count fixed at two per host frame, the two renders still
disagree about lighting, because R_RenderFrame CONSUMES dynamic-light state:

  * GL_MarkLights/GL_TransformLights bump glr.dlightframe and stamp every lit face with it;
  * GL_PushLights, on a face whose stamp is not the current one, zeroes the stamp and rebuilds
    that face's lightmap WITHOUT its dynamic light;
  * only the faces THIS render's node walk drew get re-pushed.

So the second eye re-stamped everything with a new number and any face it did not reach was
scrubbed clean by the next render that did. One face re-pushed and its neighbour not is the
wall with a bright half and a dark half; the same thing beating against the frame cadence is
the flicker.

R_SetRepeatFrame(true) marks the next render as a repeat: it rewinds glr.dlightframe to
glr.dlightframe_base, the value the host frame's first render started from, so GL_MarkLights
re-marks with the SAME stamp — same bits, same lights, same order — and nothing is
invalidated. GL_PushLights' stylecache is per-surface and consumed only by a render that
actually drew that surface, so a surface outside eye 0's node walk but inside eye 1's is
still pushed; a surface both eyes draw is pushed twice with identical content.

Second, independent bug fixed here: update_dynamic_lightmap zeroed a stale surf->dlightframe
but left surf->dlightbits, which world.c clears only on a re-mark and tess.c reads
unconditionally — a face could contribute dynamic light for lights its lightmap had just been
rebuilt without. The bits are now dropped with the stamp, except under per-pixel lighting
where they are that frame's shader light list rather than a lightmap companion.

Not addressed: shader_load_lights frustum-culls dlights per eye and glr.ppl_dlight_bits does
not encode which survived. In-game only, per-pixel-lighting only, and not a one-liner.

Apply: patch -p1 -d vendor/q2repro < this
--- a/inc/refresh/refresh.h
+++ b/inc/refresh/refresh.h
@@ -226,6 +226,7 @@
 #define R_RegisterSkin(name)    R_RegisterImage(name, IT_SKIN, IF_NONE)
 #define R_RegisterSprite(name)  R_RegisterImage(name, IT_SPRITE, IF_NONE)
 
+void    R_SetRepeatFrame(bool repeat);      // [vr overlay 0031] next frame repeats the last
 void    R_RenderFrame(const refdef_t *fd);
 void    R_LightPoint(const vec3_t origin, vec3_t light);
 
--- a/src/refresh/gl.h
+++ b/src/refresh/gl.h
@@ -139,6 +139,12 @@
     unsigned        visframe;
     unsigned        drawframe;
     unsigned        dlightframe;
+    // [vr overlay 0031] The stereo shell renders the same host frame twice, once per eye.
+    // dlightframe_base is the value dlightframe held when the FIRST render of the host frame
+    // started; repeat_frame marks the second one, which rewinds to it so GL_MarkLights
+    // re-marks with the stamps the surfaces already carry instead of invalidating them.
+    unsigned        dlightframe_base;
+    bool            repeat_frame;
     int             viewcluster1;
     int             viewcluster2;
     int             nodes_visible;
--- a/src/refresh/main.c
+++ b/src/refresh/main.c
@@ -841,6 +841,13 @@
     }
 
     return flags;
+}
+
+// [vr overlay 0031] Marks the next R_RenderFrame as a repeat of the previous one (the stereo
+// shell's second eye), so it reuses rather than consumes the frame's dynamic-light state.
+void R_SetRepeatFrame(bool repeat)
+{
+    glr.repeat_frame = repeat;
 }
 
 void R_RenderFrame(const refdef_t *fd)
@@ -850,6 +857,27 @@
     Q_assert(gl_static.world.cache || (fd->rdflags & RDF_NOWORLDMODEL));
 
     glr.drawframe++;
+
+    // [vr overlay 0031] LIGHTING IDEMPOTENCE FOR THE SECOND EYE. R_RenderFrame consumes
+    // dynamic-light state: GL_MarkLights bumps glr.dlightframe and stamps every lit face with
+    // it, and GL_PushLights treats a face whose stamp is not the current one as stale — it
+    // zeroes the stamp and rebuilds that face's lightmap WITHOUT its dynamic light. Rendering
+    // the same frame a second time for the other eye therefore re-stamped everything with a
+    // NEW number, and any face the second render's node walk did not reach kept the old one
+    // and got scrubbed clean on the next render that did reach it. One face re-pushed and its
+    // neighbour not is "the left half of the wall is bright and the right half is dark", and
+    // the whole thing beating against the frame cadence is the flicker.
+    //
+    // The repeat render rewinds to the stamp the first render of this host frame used, so
+    // GL_MarkLights re-marks with the SAME number: every face keeps a live stamp and the
+    // dlightbits ORed in are the same bits from the same lights in the same order. Faces the
+    // first eye did not draw are still pushed by the second (GL_PushLights' style cache is
+    // per-surface and only the render that actually drew a surface consumes it), and faces
+    // both eyes draw are pushed twice with identical content.
+    if (glr.repeat_frame)
+        glr.dlightframe = glr.dlightframe_base;
+    else
+        glr.dlightframe_base = glr.dlightframe;
 
     glr.fd = *fd;
     glr.ppl_bits  = 0;
--- a/src/refresh/surf.c
+++ b/src/refresh/surf.c
@@ -240,10 +240,19 @@
     add_light_styles(surf);
 
     // add all the dynamic lights
-    if (surf->dlightframe == glr.dlightframe && !gl_backend->use_per_pixel_lighting())
+    if (surf->dlightframe == glr.dlightframe && !gl_backend->use_per_pixel_lighting()) {
         add_dynamic_lights(surf);
-    else
+    } else {
+        // [vr overlay 0031] The bits belong to the stamp being dropped. This lightmap has just
+        // been rebuilt WITHOUT its dynamic lights, but dlightbits is only ever cleared on a
+        // re-mark (world.c) and is read unconditionally in tess.c — so a face left here with
+        // live bits and a dead stamp got a dynamic-light contribution the lightmap under it no
+        // longer had. Under per-pixel lighting the bits ARE this frame's light list for the
+        // shader pass rather than a lightmap stamp's companion, so they are left alone there.
         surf->dlightframe = 0;
+        if (!gl_backend->use_per_pixel_lighting())
+            surf->dlightbits = 0;
+    }
 
     // put into texture format
     put_blocklights(surf);
